  "client_view_text": " Together with a log entry timestamp, explain why it's essential to manage the order of entries in the log.\n\nA write-ahead log is a crucial component of database systems that ensures data consistency and integrity. In a database, write-ahead logging involves recording each modification to the database in a log file before the modification is actually written to the database. This log file contains a chronological record of all changes made to the database.\n\nWhen the database is shut down or crashes, a write-ahead log allows the database to recover to a consistent state by replaying the transactions recorded in the log. The log entries are replayed in the same order they were written Write a log record in the format of the pseudocode you showed earlier. 4. Write a pseudocode for a procedure that reads a record from the write-ahead log and writes it to the database. 5. 6. 7. \"\n## Step 1: Understanding the concept of a write-ahead log\nA write-ahead log (WAL) is a mechanism used by databases to ensure data consistency and durability in the face of system crashes or failures. It involves maintaining a record of all changes made to the database before they are actually applied to the database itself. This ensures that even if the database crashes or is shut down suddenly, the changes can still be applied when the system is restarted, effectively recovering the database to a consistent state.\n\n## Step 2: How a write-ahead log helps in recovery\nWhen a database writes data to disk and also adds an entry to the log, this log entry contains information about the specific data items being modified, the type of modification (insert, update, or delete), and the old and new values of the data. In the event of a crash, the database can use this log to apply the changes recorded in the log to the database, effectively recovering the data to the point just before the crash.\n\n## Step "
